---
import CaseStudyLayout from '../../layouts/CaseStudyLayout.astro';
import CaseFigure from '../../components/CaseFigure.astro';
import Arrow from '../../components/Arrow.astro';
import { local } from '../../lib/urls';

const repository = 'https://github.com/abdelrahman-fawaz18/DriveTwin';
const source = `${repository}/blob/a01abccca5f10b9da15c026e07319ccc461c23d1`;
const frontmatter = {
  slug: 'drivetwin',
  title: 'DriveTwin',
  summary: 'An electric vehicle digital twin connecting telemetry, route energy forecasting, and operator-approved charging, with a recorded browser demo.',
  category: 'Electric vehicle digital twin',
  year: '2026',
  role: 'System design, vehicle integration, backend services, forecasting, and dashboard',
  environment: 'One simulated electric bus · SUMO · OpenStreetMap roads and elevation',
  tools: 'Python, FastAPI, React, TypeScript, PostgreSQL, SQLite, MQTT, SUMO, and Docker',
  repository,
  report: local('demos/drivetwin/'),
  reportLabel: 'Try the recorded demo',
  findingLabel: 'What I built',
  finding: 'A digital twin that follows a simulated electric bus from live telemetry and energy forecasts to an approved charging stop, measured energy delivery, and resumed driving.',
  contribution: 'I designed and built the vehicle adapter, reliable telemetry pipeline, forecasting and command services, and React dashboard around a SUMO vehicle simulation.',
  leadFigure: {
    file: 'drivetwin-dashboard.png',
    alt: 'Recorded DriveTwin dashboard with a Montréal route map, battery charge, speed, motor temperature, energy forecast, and charge history.',
    caption: 'Dashboard overview from the recorded Montréal demo, paused after approval of a charging stop.',
    source: `${source}/docs/media/dashboard/README.md`,
  },
  evidence: [
    { label: 'README, features, and local setup', url: repository },
    { label: 'Architecture and component ownership', url: `${repository}/blob/main/docs/architecture.md` },
    { label: 'Engineering traces through delivery, forecasting, and charging', url: `${repository}/blob/main/docs/engineering/engineering-walkthrough.md` },
    { label: 'Simulation release verification', url: `${repository}/blob/main/docs/release/verification.md` },
    { label: 'Recorded demo verification', url: `${repository}/blob/main/docs/verification/browser-demo.md` },
  ],
};
const chargingSteps = [
  {
    file: 'drivetwin-recommendation.png',
    title: 'Review the forecast',
    alt: 'Charging recommendation with a predicted reserve breach and a proposed 12 percent departure charge.',
    caption: 'The advisor compares charging options and proposes a stop and departure target.',
    source: `${source}/docs/media/charging-story/README.md`,
  },
  {
    file: 'drivetwin-approved.png',
    title: 'Approve the stop',
    alt: 'Dashboard acknowledgment showing the recorded charging stop approval.',
    caption: 'The operator approves a specific stop. The command service checks its authority and timing.',
    source: `${source}/docs/media/charging-story/README.md`,
  },
  {
    file: 'drivetwin-measured.png',
    title: 'Verify the effect',
    alt: 'Dashboard showing delivered charging energy and the bus moving again.',
    caption: 'Later observations confirm energy transfer and the return to driving.',
    source: `${source}/docs/media/charging-story/README.md`,
  },
] as const;
---
<CaseStudyLayout frontmatter={frontmatter}>
  <h2>From vehicle readings to an operating decision</h2>
  <p>
    Monitoring a battery becomes more useful when it helps answer what happens next.
    DriveTwin tracks a simulated electric bus, estimates how much energy its remaining
    route needs, and recommends a charging stop when the forecast falls below reserve.
    An operator can approve that stop and follow the decision through to observed
    charging and resumed motion.
  </p>
  <p>
    The system uses real OpenStreetMap roads and elevation inputs, with vehicle behavior
    and battery readings supplied by SUMO. The dashboard brings together twenty signals,
    a route map, forecast history, charging decisions, and saved-trip replay.
  </p>
  <div class="demo-invitation">
    <div>
      <h3>Make the charging decision</h3>
      <p>The recorded demo pauses after thirty seconds. Approve the stop or keep driving, then follow the recorded outcome.</p>
    </div>
    <a class="button" href={local('demos/drivetwin/')}>Try the demo <Arrow /></a>
  </div>

  <h2>A charging decision, from forecast to result</h2>
  <p>
    On the Gare Centrale–Parc La Fontaine route in Montréal, the bus starts at{' '}
    <strong>11.6% charge</strong>. Its predicted minimum without charging is{' '}
    <strong>9.33%</strong>, below the <strong>10% reserve</strong>. The advisor
    recommends a charging bay and a <strong>12% departure target</strong>.
  </p>
  <div class="charging-sequence">
    {chargingSteps.map(step => (
      <section>
        <h3>{step.title}</h3>
        <CaseFigure figure={step} />
      </section>
    ))}
  </div>
  <p>
    The approved stop delivered <strong>4.192 kWh</strong>. The bus finished the
    simulated trip in <strong>11 minutes 36 seconds</strong> and stayed above reserve,
    with a measured minimum charge of <strong>10.72%</strong>.
  </p>
  <CaseFigure figure={{
    file: 'drivetwin-charging-result.svg',
    alt: 'Recorded battery charge over the trip, showing the charging interval and the predicted and measured minimum charge.',
    caption: 'Recorded charge history for the Montréal charging example. Forecast values and observed readings are labeled separately. This earlier run is distinct from the dashboard overview capture.',
    source: `${source}/docs/media/charging-story/README.md`,
  }} />
  <p class="source-note">
    <a href={`${source}/docs/verification/moving-bus-charging.md#connected-montréal-case`}>Read the connected Montréal verification</a>
  </p>

  <h2>How the system works</h2>
  <CaseFigure figure={{
    file: 'drivetwin-system-loop.svg',
    alt: 'DriveTwin system loop from a simulated bus through observations, forecasts, guarded action, and verified effects.',
    caption: 'The vehicle supplies observations, the twin estimates what comes next, and later telemetry verifies the effect of an approved action.',
    source: `${source}/docs/engineering/figures/twin-at-a-glance.svg`,
  }} />
  <p>
    A Python adapter reads SUMO through TraCI and saves observations to a SQLite queue.
    MQTT carries those observations to ingestion, which validates and commits them to
    PostgreSQL. FastAPI serves current readings, history, and forecasts to the React
    dashboard. A separate command path handles operator approval and vehicle actions.
    Docker packages the local services.
  </p>
  <h3>Keep readings until storage confirms them</h3>
  <p>
    The vehicle retains each original packet until ingestion confirms a committed
    database write. Retries preserve its identity, so a lost receipt can be recovered
    without storing a second accepted observation. In the documented one-bus outage
    check, all <strong>480 original messages and 9,600 readings</strong> reconciled
    after a five-minute delivery interruption.
  </p>
  <h3>Make the forecast depend on the route</h3>
  <p>
    The physics-based predictor accounts for elevation, stops, acceleration, rolling
    resistance, aerodynamic drag, and equipment demand. Each saved forecast identifies
    the observation, route, vehicle configuration, and assumptions used to calculate it.
    The numerical worker runs with restricted resources and returns an estimate to a
    service that validates and stores the result.
  </p>
  <h3>Check approval before issuing a command</h3>
  <p>
    The command service checks the approved stop, current vehicle data, offer expiry,
    and station conditions before committing the handoff. A durable reservation
    prevents the same request from being claimed again after an interruption.
    The journal records command acknowledgment and later observed energy transfer
    and motion as separate events.
  </p>

  <h2>Verification and operating scope</h2>
  <p>
    The geographic regression study compared seventy forecasts across ten journeys
    in Montréal, Ottawa, and the Vancouver region against SUMO battery readings.
    The largest arrival-charge error was <strong>0.471 percentage points</strong>.
    The same equations and coefficients served every route, and all seventy
    forecasts passed the study’s declared checks.
  </p>
  <CaseFigure figure={{
    file: 'drivetwin-forecast-errors.svg',
    alt: 'Maximum arrival-charge forecast error for ten geographic journeys, with the largest at 0.471 percentage points.',
    caption: 'Geographic regression results against SUMO battery readings, using the same model across ten journeys.',
    source: `${source}/docs/verification/energy-geographic.md`,
  }} />
  <p>
    The release supports one simulated bus in reviewed local configurations. The
    browser demo replays matched recorded outcomes, while the local stack runs
    the vehicle, telemetry, forecasting, and command services. Physical-bus
    validation, fleet endurance, and production deployment remain future work.
    Several older verification results retain their compact summaries after the
    original recordings were retired.
  </p>
  <p>
    The public repository includes the implementation, engineering checks, setup
    guides, and verification records. Geographic launcher trips use a separately
    installed scenario bundle. Original code and documentation use the MIT license.
    Maps retain <a href="https://www.openstreetmap.org/copyright">OpenStreetMap contributor credit and ODbL terms</a>,
    with elevation and software notices documented in the{' '}
    <a href={`${source}/docs/rights/README.md`}>source inventory</a> and{' '}
    <a href={local('licenses/drivetwin.txt')}>media notice</a>.
  </p>
</CaseStudyLayout>

<style>
  .demo-invitation {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: 24px;
    margin-top: 28px;
    padding: 24px;
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-radius: 16px;
    background: #24271f;
  }
  .demo-invitation h3 { margin: 0 0 8px; }
  .demo-invitation p { font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6; }
  .demo-invitation .button { flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 14px; }
  .charging-sequence { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(3,minmax(0,1fr)); gap: 14px; margin-top: 28px; }
  .charging-sequence h3 { font-size: 17px; margin: 0 0 10px; }
  .charging-sequence :global(.case-figure) { padding: 8px; margin: 0; }
  .charging-sequence :global(figcaption) { font-size: 12px; }
  .source-note { font-size: 13px; }
  @media(max-width:700px) {
    .demo-invitation { align-items: flex-start; flex-direction: column; }
    .charging-sequence { grid-template-columns: minmax(0,1fr); gap: 26px; }
  }
</style>
